From d1dc7677e5dd5576cefcf10f1b3733d9303c6d06 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Martin Ellis <malard@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 20:03:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [JSON-RPC][CTCPServer] Serve each connection on its own
 thread, and let no peer hold up the server (#29636)

* [JSON-RPC][CTCPServer] Serve each connection on its own thread, and let no peer hold up the server

A request handler can block in a modal dialog, and CTCPServer ran every
request on its one server thread, so every other TCP and WebSocket
client waited behind it. Announce also held the connections lock
across its whole send loop while Process held it for a request, so
the same dialog stopped every JSON-RPC notification in the
application, add-on listeners included.

Each connection's buffers are now parsed and executed on a worker of
its own. The server thread only polls, accepts and closes sockets. A
worker exits once it has drained what it was handed, so an idle or
half-sent connection holds no thread. Connections are held in
shared_ptr and Announce sends to a snapshot of the list, so a client
dropped mid-iteration stays alive until the snapshot is done with it.

The problems this fixes, and the ones it would otherwise open:

- A client that keeps sending while its request sits in a dialog is
  no longer read without limit: a connection whose unparsed input
  reaches 64 KiB is left unread until its worker catches up, so its
  receive window closes and holds the client back.
- CTCPClient::Send added send()'s -1 to an unsigned counter, so a
  send on a closed socket spun forever. It now gives up and logs.
- Send took the client lock per send() call, so a response and an
  announcement could interleave their partial writes. The lock now
  covers the whole message, and WebSocket framing, unframing and
  Disconnect take it too.
- A send to a peer that has stopped reading blocked with the client
  lock held, and on Windows shutdown() does not wake a blocked send(),
  so dropping that client or stopping the server waited on the peer.
  Client sockets are non-blocking, Send() waits for room in short
  steps and gives up once Disconnect() is called, and Deinitialize()
  shuts the sockets down before unregistering from announcements.
- A WebSocket Disconnect() took the send lock before shutting the
  socket down, so a stalled WebSocket peer held up the server thread
  and every other client. The close frame is now sent only when the
  lock is free, and goes out already framed instead of wrapped in a
  text frame; the descriptor is closed whatever state the close
  handshake reached.
- A WebSocket client over the buffer limit is closed rather than left
  open.
- An exception from a request is caught at the worker entry point and
  drops that connection instead of calling std::terminate.
- A worker that cannot be started no longer ends the server thread,
  which left the listening sockets open and no connection served.
- StopServer waits up to two seconds for running workers, so one that
  returns promptly completes before teardown.
- The announcement flags are written under the lock they are read
  under.
- A send to a peer that has gone, or to a socket Disconnect() has shut
  down, fails with EPIPE rather than raising SIGPIPE: sends pass
  MSG_NOSIGNAL, or SO_NOSIGPIPE is set where that is the mechanism, and
  the wait for room gives up once the connection is disconnecting.

TestTCPServer covers an answered and a half-sent request leaving no
worker running, and StopServer returning while an announcement is
blocked on a client that never reads.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* [JSON-RPC][CTCPServer] Close a connection a send fails on, and retry an interrupted call

A send that fails part way through a message left the connection open,
so the next message followed a truncated one and the client could not
find its place in the stream again; the connection is now closed.
A send, a wait for the socket to become writable or a read that a signal
interrupts is retried, and a read that would block on the non-blocking
socket leaves the connection open rather than treating it as closed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* [JSON-RPC][CTCPServer] Start and send nothing once the server is stopped

StopServer(true) waits at most two seconds for the connection workers and
then returns, and application shutdown carries on tearing down the services
a request reaches. A worker that outlived the wait went on executing every
request it still held and serialising and sending their responses, so a
request could run after the settings component, the JSON-RPC method table
or the GUI had gone.

The wait has to stay bounded. A request can be waiting on the thread that
stops the server: one that opened a modal dialog through the messenger is
blocked until the GUI thread's dialog loop returns, and when the quit is
processed inside that loop, the loop only returns after StopServer() does.
No wait inside StopServer() can release it. Requests blocked on queued
messages are not affected: CApplication::Stop() stops and drains the
messenger before it stops the network services.

So once StopServer() has been called a worker starts no further request,
sends nothing, and leaves the rest of its input unread. A worker that
outlives the wait finishes only the request it is already in.

The wait itself was skipped at shutdown. Network services are stopped twice,
first without waiting and then with, and StopJSONRPCServer(true) returned
early when the server thread had already exited, which it does within a
second of the first stop. Requests run on workers that outlive the server
thread, so it now always waits.

Process() no longer resets m_bStop. Create() already does, and the reset
could overwrite a stop issued as the thread started, which would lose the
stop and hang the join in StopServer(true).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* [JSON-RPC][CTCPServer] Queue output so no peer holds up another, and wait for every request at stop

A send queues the message and writes only what the socket takes. The
connection's worker waits for room after its response, and the server
thread writes the rest once the socket is writable, so the announcement
thread never waits on a peer. A peer that falls 16 MiB behind is dropped.

StopServer(true) waits for every running request, as it did when
requests ran on the server thread: its caller tears down what a request
reaches once it returns. A request that stops the server it arrived on,
as a change to the services settings does, is not waited for.

A WebSocket upgrade hands the socket and its queued output to the new
client, so an announcement still holding the old one writes nothing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

* [JSON-RPC][CTCPServer] Lay out two added lines as clang-format does

The anonymous namespace's closing brace gains its comment, and the log call for a peer that is not reading fits on two lines.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

